WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.787
Welcome back to Building
the Daily Checks Workbook.

2
00:00:02.787 --> 00:00:05.750
This is Chapter 3: what
each tab would show.

3
00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:10.502
An empty queue over a source that stopped
arriving looks exactly like a quiet night,

4
00:00:10.502 --> 00:00:13.463
and the renderer in this
chapter says which one it is.

5
00:00:13.463 --> 00:00:17.183
We'll see what Microsoft Learn
and the operator runbook give you,

6
00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:20.552
the renderer's rules, and the
read-only reachability check,

7
00:00:20.552 --> 00:00:23.500
then walk two mornings in
the fictional workspace.

8
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.599
The chapter is a draft
awaiting the owner's review,

9
00:00:26.599 --> 00:00:30.350
and the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

10
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:35.213
Why this chapter?

11
00:00:35.213 --> 00:00:38.213
A dashboard's most
dangerous state isn't red.

12
00:00:38.213 --> 00:00:40.737
It's green over a source
that stopped arriving.

13
00:00:40.737 --> 00:00:43.819
A risk queue with no rows
can mean nobody is at risk,

14
00:00:43.819 --> 00:00:47.050
or that the sign-in logs
stopped coming in last night.

15
00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:50.187
By the end of the chapter you
can show, without a tenant,

16
00:00:50.187 --> 00:00:52.896
what each tab would
display on a given morning;

17
00:00:52.896 --> 00:00:57.388
tell an operator which tabs can't be
trusted because their source went quiet;

18
00:00:57.388 --> 00:01:00.525
and prove each tab's tables
answer in a real workspace.

19
00:01:00.525 --> 00:01:02.993
It's for developers, security operations,

20
00:01:02.993 --> 00:01:06.560
and the operators who read
the workbook each morning.

21
00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:11.585
Here's what you start with.

22
00:01:11.585 --> 00:01:16.315
The operator runbook's completeness
check lists each expected table past its

23
00:01:16.315 --> 00:01:18.790
freshness limit, and
it uses a fuzzy union,

24
00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:22.647
so a missing table shows up as a
null instead of failing the query.

25
00:01:22.647 --> 00:01:26.971
Sentinel's health page gives the query
for each connector's latest failure,

26
00:01:26.971 --> 00:01:30.459
and recommends Azure Monitor
alert rules for health drifts.

27
00:01:30.459 --> 00:01:34.842
Microsoft's Intune page says compliance
is exported once a day and can

28
00:01:34.842 --> 00:01:39.148
take up to forty-eight hours, and not
to rely on a fixed completion time.

29
00:01:39.148 --> 00:01:42.883
And the Azure command line can
run a query against a workspace,

30
00:01:42.883 --> 00:01:45.290
from its log analytics extension.

31
00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.527
Here are the renderer's rules, in order.

32
00:01:51.527 --> 00:01:53.541
A link-out tab shows LINK-OUT.

33
00:01:53.541 --> 00:01:56.340
No query result at all shows NO DATA.

34
00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.180
If any source the tab depends
on is past its freshness limit,

35
00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:04.490
the tab shows STALE, and says the
rows it does show may be incomplete.

36
00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:08.165
Otherwise, any row the tab's
rule flags shows ATTENTION.

37
00:02:08.165 --> 00:02:13.040
No rows where a row is expected, like
the morning probe, shows NO DATA.

38
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:14.927
And only then, CLEAR.

39
00:02:14.927 --> 00:02:17.403
So a stale source never renders clear.

40
00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:22.630
The render carries the banner, and the
kit refuses to record it as evidence.

41
00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:27.605
What does it leave to you?

42
00:02:27.605 --> 00:02:31.692
The completeness query lists dark
tables, but the other tabs don't

43
00:02:31.692 --> 00:02:35.930
know their table is dark, so each
tab names its own sources and limits.

44
00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:41.467
A query result is just rows or no rows,
so each tab says what empty means.

45
00:02:41.467 --> 00:02:46.529
Intune's timing is variable, so the
devices tab allows forty-eight hours.

46
00:02:46.529 --> 00:02:49.164
A screenshot is easy to file as evidence,

47
00:02:49.164 --> 00:02:52.755
so the render carries the
banner and the kit refuses it.

48
00:02:52.755 --> 00:02:57.708
And nothing proves each tab's tables
answer, so the kit asks the workspace

49
00:02:57.708 --> 00:03:02.680
for each table's newest record and
count: delivering, late or missing.

50
00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:07.355
Who does what?

51
00:03:07.355 --> 00:03:10.021
Operators read the telemetry tab first,

52
00:03:10.021 --> 00:03:13.980
then their own family's tabs,
and each finding becomes a task.

53
00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:18.680
Security operations treats each dark
source as a high-severity task.

54
00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:23.118
And the security officer keeps the
reachability record, never the render.

55
00:03:23.118 --> 00:03:25.668
Lab 1 needs only saved results.

56
00:03:25.668 --> 00:03:28.948
Lab 2 needs Log Analytics
Reader on a test workspace,

57
00:03:28.948 --> 00:03:34.260
which can view and search the monitoring
data, and the check sends only queries.

58
00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:38.822
Lab 1.

59
00:03:38.822 --> 00:03:43.836
Step 1 renders the morning of November
sixteenth in the fictional workspace.

60
00:03:43.836 --> 00:03:46.902
The Entra diagnostic setting
stopped at ten past five

61
00:03:46.902 --> 00:03:49.560
the evening before, and
the sync probe didn't run.

62
00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:54.904
Telemetry flags four rows: sign-in and
audit logs are dark, the probe's table

63
00:03:54.904 --> 00:03:59.985
is dark, and the Office 365 connector's
latest health event is a failure.

64
00:03:59.985 --> 00:04:04.948
The sync, risk, emergency access
and grants tabs are all STALE.

65
00:04:04.948 --> 00:04:07.735
Step 2 shows why the risk tab isn't clear:

66
00:04:07.735 --> 00:04:13.060
its query returned no rows, but its
source stopped fourteen hours ago.

67
00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:18.998
Step 3 renders the next morning.

68
00:04:18.998 --> 00:04:21.847
The setting is back,
and telemetry is clear.

69
00:04:21.847 --> 00:04:25.540
Now the queues show what they were
hiding: one user at high risk,

70
00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:30.462
one emergency access sign-in, which turns
out to be the quarterly validation drill,

71
00:04:30.462 --> 00:04:34.660
and the review records it as a drill,
and one synchronization conflict.

72
00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:36.649
Grants and D L P alerts are clear,

73
00:04:36.649 --> 00:04:41.370
and this time clear means something,
because their sources are fresh.

74
00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:50.420
Step 4 checks each table's reachability
on the first morning, from saved answers.

75
00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:55.063
Sign-in logs, audit logs and the probe's
table are late, and the Sentinel health

76
00:04:55.063 --> 00:04:56.831
table is missing altogether:

77
00:04:56.831 --> 00:05:00.295
health monitoring was never
turned on in this workspace.

78
00:05:00.295 --> 00:05:02.208
The script exits with an error.

79
00:05:02.208 --> 00:05:06.494
Step 5 tries to record the first
morning's render as evidence.

80
00:05:06.494 --> 00:05:10.520
The kit refuses: a rendered
view is not evidence.

81
00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:17.382
Step 6 records the
reachability check instead.

82
00:05:17.382 --> 00:05:21.445
Record one, with result Fail: the
record keeps what the check found,

83
00:05:21.445 --> 00:05:26.033
with continuous monitoring and system
monitoring, and their indicators.

84
00:05:26.033 --> 00:05:29.732
A failing record is still
evidence; it's evidence of the gap.

85
00:05:29.732 --> 00:05:33.703
Step 7 runs this chapter's
tests and the evidence tests:

86
00:05:33.703 --> 00:05:36.100
sixteen passed, none failed.

87
00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:43.989
Lab 2 runs the same reachability
check against a test workspace,

88
00:05:43.989 --> 00:05:46.800
never production; the
authors haven't run it.

89
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.671
Sign in to commercial Azure,

90
00:05:48.671 --> 00:05:52.575
add the log analytics extension,
run the check, and sign out.

91
00:05:52.575 --> 00:05:57.637
Every table should be delivering; each
late or missing one becomes a task.

92
00:05:57.637 --> 00:06:02.162
The reachability record supports continuous
monitoring and system monitoring.

93
00:06:02.162 --> 00:06:05.855
The dangers you saw: a missing
connector that looks healthy,

94
00:06:05.855 --> 00:06:10.300
a missed probe showing yesterday's
green, and a render filed as evidence.

95
00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:13.210
Next, Chapter 4 deploys the workbook.
